<!-- ═══════════════════ SECTION 6: INSIGHTS & RECOMMENDATIONS ═══════════════════ -->
<section class="report-section" id="key-insights-recommendations">
  <h2>6. Key Insights &amp; Recommendations</h2>

  <h3>Top Findings</h3>

  <div class="insight-box">
    <strong>1. Steps are the single strongest lever for calorie burn.</strong> With r = 0.973 between steps and calories, any product feature that increases daily steps will reliably increase energy expenditure. This makes step-based challenges and nudges a high-ROI investment.
  </div>

  <div class="insight-box">
    <strong>2. Workout participation is high (83.1%) but flat.</strong> Participation barely varies by gender, age, or mood — the cohort is already consistently active. The opportunity lies in <em>intensity and duration</em> optimization rather than participation growth.
  </div>

  <div class="insight-box">
    <strong>3. Thursday is the engagement dead zone.</strong> Across all metrics — steps, workout rate, and calories — Thursday underperforms every other day. This represents a clear, calendar-predictable opportunity for targeted nudges.
  </div>

  <div class="insight-box">
    <strong>4. January is the weakest month; December is the strongest.</strong> Contrary to the common "New Year's resolution" bump, January shows the lowest step counts (10,176) and highest stress (5.59). December leads in steps (11,113) and calorie burn (635.7).
  </div>

  <div class="insight-box">
    <strong>5. Lifestyle indicators (stress, mood, water) are largely independent of activity.</strong> All cross-domain correlations are near zero (|r| &lt; 0.03). Stress and mood reporting may need richer measurement instruments to surface actionable signals.
  </div>

  <h3>Product &amp; Engagement Recommendations</h3>
  <ol class="rec-list">
    <li><strong>Launch "Thursday Boost" push notifications.</strong> Thursday's 3% step deficit (vs. Tuesday's peak) is consistent and actionable. A targeted mid-day nudge ("You're 500 steps behind your Tuesday pace") could lift the weakest day's engagement.</li>
    <li><strong>Redesign January onboarding around step challenges, not New Year goals.</strong> January underperformance suggests that resolution-based framing isn't sustaining engagement. Shifting to community step challenges that build gradually from a post-holiday low may be more effective.</li>
    <li><strong>Prioritize step-based features over workout-type features.</strong> Since steps drive 97.3% of calorie variance and workout type barely differentiates outcomes (calorie range across types is only 14.1 kcal), product development should focus on step-count gamification, walking route suggestions, and pedometer accuracy.</li>
    <li><strong>Develop intensity metrics beyond participation.</strong> With 83% already exercising on any given day, the next engagement frontier is workout quality. Features like "personal best" duration tracking, progressive intensity recommendations, and workout streak badges can move the needle where participation alone cannot.</li>
    <li><strong>Reassess stress and mood data collection.</strong> The uniform distributions and near-zero correlations with activity suggest the current 1–10 stress scale and 3-category mood system lack discriminatory power. Consider ecological momentary assessment (EMA) or contextual mood prompts tied to activity completions for richer wellness data.</li>
    <li><strong>Target gender-specific step campaigns based on yearly trends.</strong> Gender step trends diverge year-to-year (e.g., 7.5% gap in 2022). Segmented A/B testing of step challenges by gender could improve engagement where one group is trending down.</li>
  </ol>
</section>

